Traversing a Linked List

Traversal means visiting every node from the beginning to the end.

Use a variable called current:

current = node1

while current is not None:
    print(current.value)
    current = current.next

Output:

10
20
30
40

Important line: current = current.next means “move to the next node.”
What is head?

head means the first node of the linked list. If we know head, we can reach every other node.

Example:

head → 10 → 20 → 30 → None

Starting traversal:

current = head

Then keep moving with current = current.next.

 Inserting a Node

Suppose we have:

10 → 20 → 30 → None

We want to insert 15 between 10 and 20.

Steps:

new_node = Node(15)
new_node.next = head.next
head.next = new_node

Result:

10 → 15 → 20 → 30 → None

General pattern for inserting after current:

new_node.next = current.next
current.next = new_node

Important: connect the new node to the old next node BEFORE changing current.next, so the existing list is not lost.

Deleting a Node

Suppose we have:

10 → 20 → 30 → None

We want to delete 20.

Make 10 point directly to 30:

current.next = current.next.next

Result:

10 → 30 → None

This works by skipping the node we want to remove.

Deleting the First Node

If the first node is 10:

head → 10 → 20 → 30 → None

Move head to the next node:

head = head.next

Result:

head → 20 → 30 → None

Most Important Things to Remember

head = first node of the linked list.

current = the node we are currently visiting.

current.value = get the current node’s value.

current.next = get the next node.

current = current.next = move forward by one node.

new_node.next = current.next = connect the new node to the rest of the list.

current.next = new_node = connect current to the new node.

current.next = current.next.next = skip/delete the next node.

head = head.next = remove the first node.
